+{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5","id":"msg_011CdP55KjTwHHgDBLK31Hs3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01J6eN4soagkidsccPjhrh6s","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/tmp/p_tests.txt","content":"Write a TypeScript vitest test file. Respond with ONLY the TypeScript source code, no explanations, no markdown fences. Follow this specification EXACTLY.\n\nThe file starts with:\n\nimport { describe, it, expect } from 'vitest';\nimport { solveToggle, solveCounter } from '../src/index.js';\nimport type { Machine } from '../src/index.js';\n\nAPI under test:\n- solveToggle(machine: {target: boolean[]; buttons: number[][]}): number[] | null\n  N lights all start false; pressing button i toggles each light listed in buttons[i]; each button used at most once. Returns the ascending-sorted set of button indices whose combined toggles produce target, choosing fewest buttons, ties broken by lexicographically smallest sorted array. Returns [] when target is all false. Returns null when impossible.\n- solveCounter(lights: number[], buttons: number[][], modulus: number): number[] | null\n  N counters start at 0; pressing button i adds 1 mod modulus to each counter listed in buttons[i]; button i pressed result[i] times (0..modulus-1). Returns the press-count array with minimum total sum, ties broken by lexicographically smallest array. Returns null when impossible.\n\nInclude these helper functions at top level (implement them exactly as described):\n\n1. function mulberry32(seed: number): () => number\n   Standard mulberry32 PRNG returning floats in [0,1).\n\n2. function applyToggle(n: number, buttons: number[][], presses: number[]): boolean[]\n   Start with Array(n).fill(false); for each button index p of presses, for each light L of buttons[p], flip state[L]. Return state.\n\n3. function bruteToggle(target: boolean[], buttons: number[][]): number[] | null\n   Exhaustive reference. M = buttons.length (assume M <= 16). For mask from 0 to 2^M - 1: build presses = indices of set bits ascending; simulate with applyToggle; if the state equals target elementwise, it is a candidate. Among candidates keep the best: fewer indices wins; on equal length, lexicographic elementwise comparison of the ascending index arrays (first differing element smaller wins). Return best or null.\n\n4. function applyCounter(n: number, buttons: number[][], x: number[], mod: number): number[]\n   Start with Array(n).fill(0); for each button i, for each counter c of buttons[i], state[c] = (state[c] + x[i]) % mod. Return state.\n\n5. function bruteCounter(lights: number[], buttons: number[][], mod: number): number[] | null\n   Exhaustive reference (assume mod^M small). Recursively enumerate every x in {0..mod-1}^M in lexicographic order; simulate with applyCounter; if it equals lights elementwise it is a candidate. Keep the best: smaller sum of entries wins; on equal sum, lexicographically smaller array wins. Return best or null.\n\n6. function cmpArrays(a: number[], b: number[]): number\n   Elementwise compare, then by length.\n\nThen these test suites:\n\ndescribe('solveToggle basics'):\n- 'task example is unsolvable': target [true,true,false,true], buttons [[0,1],[1,2,3],[0,3]] -> expect null.\n- 'all-off target needs no presses': target [false,false,false], buttons [[0],[1,2]] -> expect [].\n- 'single button': target [true,false], buttons [[0]] -> expect [0].\n- 'no buttons, nonzero target': target [true], buttons [] -> expect null.\n- 'zero lights': target [], buttons [[],[]] -> expect [].\n- 'duplicate light index in a button cancels': target [true], buttons [[0,0]] -> expect null; and target [false], buttons [[0,0]] -> expect [].\n- 'prefers fewer buttons': target [true,true], buttons [[0],[1],[0,1]] -> expect [2].\n- 'lex tie-break': target [true,true], buttons [[0,1],[0],[1]] where pressing [0] and pressing [1,2] both work -> expect [0].\n- 'identical buttons tie-break': target [true], buttons [[0],[0]] -> expect [0].\n\ndescribe('solveToggle randomized vs brute force'):\n- One test that loops 150 iterations with rng = mulberry32(12345). Each iteration: n = 1 + floor(rng()*5) lights, m = floor(rng()*9) buttons; each button = random subset of lights (include each light with probability 0.5); target = random booleans (each true with probability 0.5). Compute expected = bruteToggle(target, buttons) and actual = solveToggle({target, buttons}); expect(actual).toEqual(expected).\n\ndescribe('solveToggle performance'):\n- 'handles 40 buttons and 40 lights quickly': rng = mulberry32(999). n = 40, m = 40 buttons, each a random subset (probability 0.5 per light). Choose secret = every button index with rng() < 0.5. target = applyToggle(n, buttons, secret). const t0 = Date.now(); const res = solveToggle({target, buttons}); expect Date.now() - t0 to be less than 5000. expect res not null; expect applyToggle(n, buttons, res!) toEqual target; expect res!.length <= secret.length.\n- 'unsolvable 40-button instance is fast': same construction with rng = mulberry32(777) but buttons never touch light 39 (random subsets of lights 0..38 only) and target has target[39] = true (other entries random booleans). Expect null and runtime under 5000 ms.\n\ndescribe('solveCounter basics'):\n- 'simple unique solution': lights [2,3], buttons [[0],[1]], mod 5 -> expect [2,3].\n- 'shared counter': lights [3], buttons [[0],[0]], mod 5 -> minimum total is 3 pressed as lexicographically smallest [0,3] -> expect [0,3].\n- 'zero target': lights [0,0], buttons [[0],[1]], mod 7 -> expect [0,0].\n- 'no buttons, nonzero target': lights [1], buttons [], mod 3 -> expect null.\n- 'untouched counter makes it unsolvable': lights [1,1], buttons [[0]], mod 3 -> expect null.\n- 'duplicate counter index adds twice': lights [4], buttons [[0,0]], mod 5 -> pressing once adds 2, so need x with 2x=4 mod 5, minimal x=2 -> expect [2].\n- 'button affecting nothing stays 0': lights [1], buttons [[],[0]], mod 3 -> expect [0,1].\n- 'mod 2 behaves like toggle': lights [1,1,0], buttons [[0,1],[1,2],[0,2]], mod 2 -> expect [1,0,0].\n\ndescribe('solveCounter randomized vs brute force'):\n- One test looping 120 iterations with rng = mulberry32(4242). Each iteration: mod = [2,3,5][floor(rng()*3)]; n = 1 + floor(rng()*4); m = floor(rng()*5); if mod**m > 4000 skip (continue); each button = random subset of counters (probability 0.5); lights = random values, each floor(rng()*mod). expected = bruteCounter(lights, buttons, mod); actual = solveCounter(lights, buttons, mod); expect(actual).toEqual(expected).\n\ndescribe('solveCounter performance'):\n- 'handles 40 buttons, 40 counters, mod 97': rng = mulberry32(31337). n = 40, m = 40, each button = random subset (probability 0.4 per counter); secret = Array of m values floor(rng()*10); lights = applyCounter(n, buttons, secret, 97). Time solveCounter(lights, buttons, 97): result non-null, runtime < 5000 ms, applyCounter(n, buttons, res!, 97) toEqual lights, and sum of res! <= sum of secret.\n- 'duplicate-heavy instance': n = 6, buttons = 40 buttons cycling through the patterns [[0],[1],[2],[3],[4],[5],[0,1]] repeated (buttons[i] = patterns[i % 7]); lights = [5,4,3,2,1,0]; mod 97. Time it, expect < 5000 ms, non-null, applyCounter matches lights, and every entry of the result is between 0 and 96.\n\nUse expect(...).toEqual(...) for deep equality and expect(x).toBeNull() / not.toBeNull() where appropriate. Non-null assertions (res!) are fine after expecting not null. Do not use any. TypeScript strict mode.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":268,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10663,"cache_read_input_tokens":32801,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10663},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cf820e2e-9f07-4ff6-